### 7. Performed baseline QC and created a baseline-corrected DCM-ready file

The manual grand mean showed a residual baseline offset when checked numerically:

| Measure before final correction | Mean absolute baseline |
|---|---:|
| Standard | 17.3594 |
| Pitch Deviant | 17.6286 |
| Pitch Deviant - Standard | 12.1100 |

This did not mean that preprocessing had failed, but for DCM it is cleaner to use a file where the final grand mean is re-centred to the -100 to 0 ms baseline. Therefore, a baseline-corrected copy was created:

```text
/Users/klauskrause/Documents/MATLAB/ds004837_controls_spm/DCM_Day1_GrandMean/grandmean_20HC_standard_pitch_manual_bc.mat
```

Baseline verification after correction showed:

| Measure after final correction | Mean absolute baseline |
|---|---:|
| Standard | 0.00000004 |
| Pitch Deviant | 0.00000005 |
| Pitch Deviant - Standard | 0.00000006 |

The baseline-corrected file was verified as type `evoked`, with two conditions, 102 channels, 200 Hz sampling rate, -100 to 300 ms time window, and no bad channels marked.

### 8. Visual QC of the baseline-corrected grand mean

#### GFP: Standard vs Pitch Deviant

Shows overall sensor-level response strength for the two conditions. The response rises after sound onset, with broadly similar Standard and Pitch Deviant morphology and stronger Pitch Deviant activity at several post-stimulus periods. Judgement: acceptable and useful.

#### GFP: Pitch Deviant - Standard

Shows when the two conditions differ most strongly across sensors. The difference is low during baseline and rises clearly after sound onset, with a strong early effect around 100-120 ms and a later effect around 240-280 ms. Judgement: good evidence that the Pitch Deviant contrast is present.

#### Butterfly: Standard

Shows all 102 channel waveforms for the Standard condition. The baseline is centred near zero and the post-stimulus activity is structured across many channels rather than one obvious rogue channel. Judgement: good.

#### Butterfly: Pitch Deviant

Shows all 102 channel waveforms for the Pitch Deviant condition. The baseline is also clean and the evoked response is somewhat stronger/more complex than Standard in the expected post-stimulus period. Judgement: good.

#### Butterfly: Pitch Deviant - Standard

Shows channel-level difference waves. The pre-stimulus difference is small compared with the post-stimulus response, and the main difference is distributed across channels around 100-150 ms. Judgement: acceptable and not obviously dominated by a single bad sensor.

Overall visual QC judgement: the baseline-corrected grand mean is acceptable for proceeding to DCM model setup. The Standard and Pitch Deviant responses are clearly structured, the Pitch Deviant contrast is present around 100-150 ms, and the difference is not obviously dominated by one rogue channel. A strong late response around 250-300 ms should be documented and kept in mind when fitting over the full -100 to 300 ms DCM window.

